{{!
    @template enrol_apply/application_navigation

    Links to the applications either side of the one being reviewed.

    Both links carry the applicant's name as VISIBLE text, so neither needs a title or an
    aria-label: the visible text is already the accessible name, and an aria-label repeating it
    would only override the name with a copy of itself. The arrow beside each is decorative and
    is hidden from a screen reader rather than merely unlabelled - pix_icon's constructor sets
    aria-hidden on any icon whose alt is empty, which is what giving the pix helper no third
    argument produces. Verified in the rendered markup on 5.2, not assumed.

    The markup is shaped on mod_book's own navigation but deliberately does NOT copy its
    btn-previous and btn-next class names. Measured: those two are defined nowhere in core - only
    in mod/book/styles.css, under .path-mod-book - so on this page they would style nothing while
    reading as though they carried mod_book's behaviour. What they DO carry there is a right-to-
    left flip for the chevrons, which core does not apply to any icon but fa-question; this
    plugin's own stylesheet carries that rule instead, keyed on the wrapper class below.

    The whole landmark is omitted when there is nothing to link to at all, so a lone application
    reviewed by an operator with no queue does not leave an empty navigation region for a screen
    reader to announce and step into. It is NOT gated on the neighbours alone: the queue link has
    to survive a queue of one, which is exactly when a reader most needs the way back.

    Context variables required for this template:
    * navlabel string - accessible name of the navigation landmark
    * hasnav boolean - whether there is anything to link to at all
    * previous object - the earlier application, absent at the start of the queue, carrying:
      * title string - the link's whole text, naming the applicant, plain spelling
      * url string - review page of that application
    * next object - the later application, absent at the end of the queue, same two keys
    * hasqueueurl boolean - whether this operator may open the queue this walk runs in
    * queuelabel string - text of the link back to that queue
    * queueurl string - the queue's own page

    Example context (json):
    {
        "navlabel": "Applications awaiting a decision",
        "hasnav": true,
        "hasqueueurl": true,
        "queuelabel": "All applications",
        "queueurl": "https://example.com/enrol/apply/manage.php?id=7",
        "previous": {
            "title": "Previous: Alice Anderson",
            "url": "https://example.com/enrol/apply/manage.php?userenrol=41"
        },
        "next": {
            "title": "Next: Bob Brown",
            "url": "https://example.com/enrol/apply/manage.php?userenrol=43"
        }
    }
}}
{{#hasnav}}
<nav class="enrol_apply-applicationnav mb-3" aria-label="{{navlabel}}">
    <div class="d-flex flex-wrap align-items-center gap-2">
        {{#previous}}
            <a class="btn btn-link ps-0" href="{{url}}">
                {{#pix}} i/previous, core {{/pix}}
                {{title}}
            </a>
        {{/previous}}
        {{#hasqueueurl}}
            <a class="btn btn-link mx-auto" href="{{queueurl}}">{{queuelabel}}</a>
        {{/hasqueueurl}}
        {{#next}}
            <a class="btn btn-link ms-auto pe-0" href="{{url}}">
                {{title}}
                {{#pix}} i/next, core {{/pix}}
            </a>
        {{/next}}
    </div>
</nav>
{{/hasnav}}
